following gives the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2})=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x).$\n\n![](images/ef/efac5abdc5ec66c794f01c53d14b10f51ff527699eae40d2f6d5802f7b51a15e.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)).$\n\n![](images/76/76182965d505e8c87d15b65569c6a67f9e6e0baedbbf18ece2ff59feb85efd69.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2456098c-d6ed-5d85-a1a1-69ad54f02216" :knowledge-point/title "Other Cases of Graphing Transformations Containing Two Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f1d40f-8634-5477-87da-fcd08a8d8fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b69a31c547211f3e80c0d8dbd4628f7afffc2301a73ff90b3c8e07d1e68e7c98.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525216} {:answer/id #uuid "1bad2f7e-705f-57d6-a8b6-b20109d9c1db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/25b62dd5cbfd65c8192c821209855caf323727702053d74e5eace9c9d780ba73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525264} {:answer/id #uuid "32b8daf4-42e5-5732-9e45-6ae67219afd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fb/fbef9f37f932d01b14e99f6688377c9f9ba78c431edc1489e860247235ff3b51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525331} {:answer/id #uuid "af784d02-f28c-5372-b31b-6d0b895f13fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1d/1d8f1fd494ff387282dc910b348a804aa3d6299f340b7571ce129f6f8006570c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525649} {:answer/id #uuid "b37d24d3-58f3-5c1f-b13b-7316ccdf3eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3f103600a795929e3e3cb37a3d8ecf35b66adf7df9b9c0364153432e5b38ab1a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32b8daf4-42e5-5732-9e45-6ae67219afd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fb/fbef9f37f932d01b14e99f6688377c9f9ba78c431edc1489e860247235ff3b51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525331} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b3e4094-b77b-52a0-aa06-be77391d633f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "578fd19d-050c-5dea-a9f2-ce6923cbde5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-87271" :question/problem "![](images/dd/ddb1bce0c22f83b4333c4552081cb527dde48da8dfafddb392c1cc3db12c702d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(3x)+3?$" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(3x)+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(3x).$\n\n![](images/fc/fc63b6078542e010a6203b139e59c82132e08038e713a295a39377c137f5fbaa.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(3x)+3.$\n\n![](images/5e/5ee1df4a6b8624f25d4de8915d38fe12eb591bd08ed183879d8e94d4721e3a76.png)"}]}
